{"versions":[{"path":"versions/19fdbe5e-1ff4-40c4-948a-c43ad4706924/b987a47e911af6397ffbfdb2e9d491436e325b884e4181fcaedd7ac0fc2beec4.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"19fdbe5e-1ff4-40c4-948a-c43ad4706924","revision":"sha256:b987a47e911af6397ffbfdb2e9d491436e325b884e4181fcaedd7ac0fc2beec4","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"To solve a problem by dynamic programming instead of recursions, the key approach is to store the results of computations for the subproblems so that we only have to compute each different subproblem once. Those solutions can be stored in an array or a hash table."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":true,"option_id":"6de1ca0a-45e9-4cd2-b4ed-aae5d2921b67"},{"type":"text","content":"False","correct":false,"option_id":"5cdacdce-662c-4a30-b5eb-e8818a2b7d8d"}]}}},{"path":"versions/9dc48830-1808-448e-9e66-71cb43ba70b3/7fcd2c9f53c885cd02acbe60a256a3c87743e42c74c211f1aeccbf6d53db620d.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"9dc48830-1808-448e-9e66-71cb43ba70b3","revision":"sha256:7fcd2c9f53c885cd02acbe60a256a3c87743e42c74c211f1aeccbf6d53db620d","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"We can tell that there must be a lot of redundant calculations during the exhaustive search for the matrix multiplication problem, because the search work load is the Catalan number, yet there are only ___ different sub-problems"},{"type":"image","src":"../../assets/d6bb43b3da58d8452dee2e71e4fda114261d77c0f9eee38d71de32c46d869932.png"},{"type":"text","content":"."}],"options":[{"type":"image","src":"../../assets/85995fa4b4a08502c25a91d7820389eabe107826ff7739e0a05369b529dae27d.png","correct":false,"option_id":"15f14ffc-db48-4e4d-89ab-13fdaae8ee68"},{"type":"image","src":"../../assets/bcee34aeec5eb91a31c2677805f2dcc481b3bd78fdcccf6304aa221fdb86ecb8.png","correct":true,"option_id":"f087958a-50b5-4af2-a19e-2ad5e6d0269a"},{"type":"image","src":"../../assets/542bac19c5c12993bf3afaa63460fcef026f70382805de8b4e8d55b19b779f8c.png","correct":false,"option_id":"81e31fb8-0552-4e7a-b42d-acc1ea00a098"},{"type":"image","src":"../../assets/8e199bb373018ce6df69cb87fa2201ae4f6a952d65f64ae244c99d34d58ac98c.png","correct":false,"option_id":"8a2afb67-064b-457b-89e7-9cd84901a3f2"}]}}},{"path":"versions/4d83d033-85d5-43e7-978e-3d46caab5e7e/aa2036e381a1bb769c7d78cb0ff72df79c500ec46e38c4c31798a280e6380933.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"4d83d033-85d5-43e7-978e-3d46caab5e7e","revision":"sha256:aa2036e381a1bb769c7d78cb0ff72df79c500ec46e38c4c31798a280e6380933","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"The root of an optimal binary search tree always contains the key with the highest search probability."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":false,"option_id":"4826a2db-85d4-4fb0-8910-d20d6e98ac69"},{"type":"text","content":"False","correct":true,"option_id":"278a1e3b-2bdc-4917-ba5a-20c41e57d839"}]}}},{"path":"versions/18518823-5bcc-4105-978e-b0b46a1afaa7/4d79937169e07325835aef2a51c40959084011d7a576ce7aa0567f89d265a351.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"18518823-5bcc-4105-978e-b0b46a1afaa7","revision":"sha256:4d79937169e07325835aef2a51c40959084011d7a576ce7aa0567f89d265a351","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"Why doesn't Floyd algorithm work if there are negative-cost cycles?"}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"Because Floyd didn't like negative numbers.","correct":false,"option_id":"37990564-d99d-4b48-8c82-013ca92032b8"},{"type":"text","content":"Because Floyd algorithm will terminate after finite steps, yet the shortest distance is negative infinity if there is a negative-cost cycle.","correct":true,"option_id":"129742d1-d608-46fa-b2c3-cab7de7b8534"},{"type":"text","content":"Because Floyd algorithm will fall into infinite loops.","correct":false,"option_id":"f2df30d0-acd3-4299-9e7f-44436b2526b4"},{"type":"text","content":"Because a negative-cost cycle will result in a negative D[i][i], yet Floyd algorithm can only accept positive weights.","correct":false,"option_id":"e91b9468-c6ae-44c9-83e1-dfad1e415ecc"}]}}},{"path":"versions/39e0469a-c356-4a29-8342-f3af1861457f/60b534d62497e16ca070b743412053d20e1fd125037d8c1c04c900c5d727aa00.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"39e0469a-c356-4a29-8342-f3af1861457f","revision":"sha256:60b534d62497e16ca070b743412053d20e1fd125037d8c1c04c900c5d727aa00","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"In dynamic programming, we derive a recurrence relation for the solution to one subproblem in terms of solutions to other subproblems. To turn this relation into a bottom up dynamic programming algorithm, we need an order to fill in the solution cells in a table, such that all needed subproblems are solved before solving a subproblem. Among the following relations, which one is impossible to be computed?"}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"A(i,j)=min(A(i−1,j),A(i,j−1),A(i−1,j−1))","correct":false,"option_id":"2fe25c18-2506-496b-b6a3-b2910c9a81ca"},{"type":"text","content":"A(i,j)=F(A(min{i,j}−1,min{i,j}−1),A(max{i,j}−1,max{i,j}−1))","correct":false,"option_id":"62a70bf9-6fde-4753-a803-4b4c1cb5c012"},{"type":"text","content":"A(i,j)=F(A(i,j−1),A(i−1,j−1),A(i−1,j+1))","correct":false,"option_id":"12cf95fb-3080-42b8-91c6-98ed5aeec1c9"},{"type":"text","content":"A(i,j)=F(A(i−2,j−2),A(i+2,j+2))","correct":true,"option_id":"114a5c32-9fb8-4965-9f8f-acb859f32aa1"}]}}}]}